      "question": {
        ":question/id": "f607f504-9528-5b40-8c4d-0cc7f62c9c02",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{8x+4}+\\frac{1}{8x-4})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{8x+4}+\\frac{1}{8x-4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{8x+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{8x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|8x+4|+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|8x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|(8x+4)(8x-4)|+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(K|(8x+4)(8x-4)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm.",
